<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty</p><h1 class="enc-title">Klaipėda Convention</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1924">1924</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Memel Convention</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/klaipeda">Klaipėda</a> Convention was an international agreement signed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in May 1924 between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. It recognised Lithuanian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> over the Klaipėda Region, taken by Lithuania in the revolt of 1923, while granting the region wide autonomy with its own elected parliament and directorate. The port was to serve the trade of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neman">Neman</a> basin, and the arrangement lasted until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> took the region in 1939.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/klaipeda-convention.jpg" alt="Klaipėda Convention" width="320" height="244" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Memelland_1923-1939.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
